            <div xml:id="d36">

                <pb facs="0133" n="31" sameAs="frus:frus1935v01/pg31"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">500.A15A4 General Committee (Arms)/117:
                    Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the American Delegate</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic">Wilson</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1935-03-07T12:00:00-05:00">March 7,
                            1935—noon</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>480. No. 91 March 5, 6 p.m., from Embassy, London. The objections to inspection
                    on the spot, which Simon set forth in his conversation with Atherton, would
                    appear to be based upon the false assumption that the inspection provided for in
                    the Draft Articles is intended to cover stocks in reserve and arms in possession
                    of the armed forces of the State. Does the British Delegation thoroughly
                    understand that such is not the case and that the inspection provided for covers
                    arms only in the course of manufacture and up to the time of their delivery?</p>
                <p>Repeat to London.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
